comments:
@eerhardt I think maybe you are picturing that systemd is running inside the container. That's not the case; systemd runs *outside* the container and the notify socket is proxied into the container via the `--sd-notify=container` flag in the docker/podman run command.

It is possible to have docker/podman manage notifications by itself with `--sd-notify=conmon`, but this is less preferable because conmon will report to systemd that the service is "active" as soon as the container has booted and the containerised process (in my case an ASP.NET webapi) has started. However, the webapi might not be ready to serve requests yet at this point. By proxying the notify socket into the container from the host machine, we can let ASP.NET notify systemd more correctly of the service's readiness.

(In my use case at least), systemd manages the lifecycle of the service, while podman manages the environment (runtime dependencies, etc). They have some overlap in that both have various security sandboxing capabilities, but for the most part their purposes are orthogonal.
